  'research-methods-in-anthropology',
  null,
  'Research Methods in Anthropology',
  'Research Methods in Anthropology',
  'Research methods in anthropology are a disciplined way of producing accountable knowledge about human life, combining interpretive, comparative, historical, and empirical habits of inquiry. Students design questions and fieldsites, practice ethics and reflexivity, and collect evidence through observation, interviews, elicitation, surveys, archives, and material or digital methods, then analyze, steward, and write that evidence into a feasible project.',
  $rmanjson${"slug": "research-methods-in-anthropology", "title": "Research Methods in Anthropology", "description": "Research methods in anthropology are a disciplined way of producing accountable knowledge about human life, combining interpretive, comparative, historical, and empirical habits of inquiry. Students design questions and fieldsites, practice ethics and reflexivity, and collect evidence through observation, interviews, elicitation, surveys, archives, and material or digital methods, then analyze, steward, and write that evidence into a feasible project.", "topics": [{"id": "6d6d98fe-6994-45ef-8564-9717f5ad89c0", "type": "topic", "title": "Anthropological Research as Craft, Science, and Public Practice", "description": "This opening topic frames research methods as a disciplined way of producing accountable knowledge about human life. Students learn that anthropology combines interpretive, comparative, historical, and empirical habits of inquiry.", "children": [{"id": "061cdf1e-5573-4ba9-a8bf-e7297454815e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Four-field and applied research logics", "children": [{"id": "aa9f3ef9-8ad0-4e6c-95e2-68d66b32c60d", "type": "concept", "title": "Research traditions across anthropology subfields"}, {"id": "266d05e3-dc66-40e2-abcc-3be4afc16029", "type": "concept", "title": "Humanistic interpretation and social-scientific evidence"}, {"id": "ff8cdb07-96b1-468c-9578-ff10622b1261", "type": "concept", "title": "Academic, public, policy, and community audiences"}], "description": "Students see how methods vary across cultural, linguistic, archaeological, biological, public, and applied anthropology."}, {"id": "ad57ce78-ed84-4157-9d10-a3baf8d495fe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence, interpretation, and uncertainty", "children": [{"id": "91a24dca-47ab-4c25-8738-c35679747e3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation, interview, archive, artifact, survey, and digital evidence"}, {"id": "957f27d4-10ef-40a3-827b-4c0629c45c5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Triangulation and competing explanations"}, {"id": "61b87ee7-274e-4267-81aa-9a816664e65d", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits, bias, and uncertainty in field-based knowledge"}], "description": "The point is to treat anthropological claims as arguments built from situated evidence."}, {"id": "837ec922-0cfc-486c-8512-aa0efc544e9e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Methods as relationships", "children": [{"id": "6b5b8cea-8e79-495a-b839-64b07363fb2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Trust, rapport, access, and refusal"}, {"id": "f44985b2-8b62-4f1a-8b8a-65dfcdf0c04a", "type": "concept", "title": "Researcher responsibilities before and after fieldwork"}, {"id": "ac35fb8e-3fdf-421c-b3eb-e302f12933d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Reciprocity, accountability, and long-term consequences"}], "description": "Students learn that anthropology is shaped by relationships with people, places, institutions, and histories."}]}, {"id": "4d6ad2ef-f650-4e80-af49-d32e3dec2e4d", "type": "topic", "title": "Research Questions, Literature Review, and Theory", "description": "A strong methods project begins before data collection. This topic teaches students to develop meaningful questions, read scholarly conversations, and connect theory to research design.", "children": [{"id": "6b069575-12d5-4cc2-88cd-ee7e04ef00dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Question design", "children": [{"id": "7b87feef-9602-4789-bd84-bd1f5b368255", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive, interpretive, comparative, and explanatory questions"}, {"id": "f544beed-682b-4dd5-a567-1ddbad1cd43b", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope, feasibility, and ethical stakes"}, {"id": "c8d43706-12f8-4941-b2d7-fb57e0f27475", "type": "concept", "title": "Conceptual definitions and operational decisions"}], "description": "Students learn to move from broad curiosity to researchable problems."}, {"id": "e80a05d6-d243-4cc4-ad73-a8f1be8d2a33", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Literature review strategy", "children": [{"id": "b6b9b350-1abe-4d15-b409-a1df61404968", "type": "concept", "title": "AnthroSource, library databases, bibliographies, and citation chaining"}, {"id": "1dadefe9-4b46-4dc0-91a0-0c521c8f830c", "type": "concept", "title": "Classic texts versus current debates"}, {"id": "a642b846-729d-4dc4-9f70-9156e22f2425", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliographies and synthesis matrices"}], "description": "The gist is to find the conversation a project is entering."}, {"id": "bd73595a-c721-46e9-b92a-b1c1135d5dfb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory-method alignment", "children": [{"id": "f3e3de5b-c133-4410-84f0-9ada418c0567", "type": "concept", "title": "Practice theory, political economy, interpretive anthropology, feminism, postcolonial theory"}, {"id": "efa920b7-f744-4f7f-ad70-da480151a967", "type": "concept", "title": "Emic and etic framing"}, {"id": "c7ac46fe-374b-4af0-8f09-ecc9b7043c3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as lens, not decoration"}], "description": "Students practice choosing methods that actually answer the theoretical question."}]}, {"id": "2f4a7000-6863-4af2-9766-070048cbde7e", "type": "topic", "title": "Research Design, Sampling, and Fieldsite Selection", "description": "This topic turns an idea into a defensible study plan. Students learn how to select sites, participants, cases, and methods with purpose rather than convenience alone.", "children": [{"id": "167122b6-9d9b-4ac7-a091-c553b453521a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fieldsite selection", "children": [{"id": "3728a3df-a603-488e-bd29-2ade62025fb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-site, multi-sited, virtual, archival, and institutional fieldsites"}, {"id": "d3db0297-d003-4f10-b1f0-ab11f7723c85", "type": "concept", "title": "Gatekeepers, permissions, and access politics"}, {"id": "9e8c3516-7902-4e2a-adbe-faacab8f81a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Feasibility, safety, language, and time constraints"}], "description": "Students learn why sites are constructed through questions, access, relationships, and scale."}, {"id": "db667ae6-d1e5-4de1-ac7e-e90882eb1ee0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling in anthropology", "children": [{"id": "de589348-7687-474f-9348-bd018027e78b", "type": "concept", "title": "Purposive, snowball, quota, convenience, and respondent-driven sampling"}, {"id": "5073f8e6-cd21-4c55-8340-fd69a3a66c98", "type": "concept", "title": "Key informants, cases, events, and documents"}, {"id": "a0cc97b5-bbcb-41e5-b369-46bfeaa4f5e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Sample size, saturation, and variation"}], "description": "The point is to distinguish statistical representativeness from theoretical and purposive sampling."}, {"id": "77fdae17-272a-41bb-a1e8-398bdaaf57c2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mixed-method design", "children": [{"id": "7d633d15-4ef8-4ed6-9ab1-91eb2b746587", "type": "concept", "title": "Sequential, convergent, and embedded designs"}, {"id": "e510dad9-28da-4fa4-b210-25dc67c95cf3", "type": "concept", "title": "When surveys complement ethnography"}, {"id": "a264b0b1-849b-4840-830b-9c7250dcdbbc", "type": "concept", "title": "Integration plans and points of triangulation"}], "description": "Students learn how qualitative and quantitative elements can reinforce each other."}]}, {"id": "fb1f74c7-7149-4a99-b447-105c4b600476", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethics, Consent, IRB, and Risk", "description": "Anthropological methods require ethical judgment, not checkbox compliance. This topic prepares students to protect participants, communities, data, and themselves.", "children": [{"id": "53c40f9b-e08c-4128-94b9-a12fd744e4b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Informed consent in context", "children": [{"id": "db3740d5-91c7-4f22-9160-b076311a7174", "type": "concept", "title": "Written, oral, implied, and process consent"}, {"id": "c2fdcd51-fda5-445d-aad1-b1e8fb1d2ad9", "type": "concept", "title": "Language access and comprehension"}, {"id": "e0a9660c-13b8-4b69-8fa1-a11b1201c2cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Power, vulnerability, and the right to withdraw"}], "description": "Students learn that consent is a continuing relationship rather than a signed form alone."}, {"id": "a17eaaa9-5f7b-42cb-aa95-e8455a028da9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "IRB and human subjects protection", "children": [{"id": "9ea6b3d0-cebb-44cd-a775-edaf4336355b", "type": "concept", "title": "Belmont principles and Common Rule basics"}, {"id": "06bdde20-f08f-4754-90d8-45460dd2d645", "type": "concept", "title": "Exempt, expedited, and full-board review concepts"}, {"id": "094213c8-a448-47ca-abbe-5367e9768c81", "type": "concept", "title": "Protocol writing, amendments, adverse events, and confidentiality plans"}], "description": "The goal is to understand regulatory review while recognizing its limits in fieldwork."}, {"id": "2f226076-1c6b-45f0-a555-5aa80144b0cc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anthropological ethics beyond compliance", "children": [{"id": "4dfc114a-fbaf-48b0-b332-ee016323599d", "type": "concept", "title": "Do no harm, transparency, and avoiding secret research"}, {"id": "5e66cd97-b680-4656-84a8-519fa82f6bd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Community review, Indigenous data sovereignty, and cultural protocols"}, {"id": "8e9eaa14-8b32-455e-9d9f-1441d64ae890", "type": "concept", "title": "Researcher safety, trauma, and fieldwork boundaries"}], "description": "Students consider harms and obligations that regulations may not fully capture."}]}, {"id": "8d1448d5-a804-4395-bbb9-e6cdcdb10c2d", "type": "topic", "title": "Reflexivity, Positionality, and Power", "description": "This topic teaches students to examine how knowledge is shaped by who asks, who answers, and under what conditions. Reflexivity becomes a method for improving, not weakening, research.", "children": [{"id": "394364e1-3577-4eca-92be-729d293b2342", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Positionality statements", "children": [{"id": "fca24daa-f01e-4729-83c5-5782f8ae1118", "type": "concept", "title": "Insider, outsider, and partial insider positions"}, {"id": "bc5399be-cbe0-4f29-a5b3-37d960b26e3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, class, gender, nationality, language, religion, and institutional power"}, {"id": "0735b93f-148c-44b1-bfa6-c63d6a1f4eb1", "type": "concept", "title": "Positionality as analytic evidence"}], "description": "Students learn to describe social location and research relationships without making the project self-absorbed."}, {"id": "6b5b16c6-b13f-475b-b698-f9d16e617100", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflexive practice", "children": [{"id": "f07bdef4-82e1-4776-9381-07f9329752df", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflexive journaling and analytic memos"}, {"id": "0f3a7e82-043a-4ab1-ba56-56d9518a4665", "type": "concept", "title": "Emotional labor, discomfort, and surprise"}, {"id": "157b8a1e-9efc-4355-87da-04a4658ccf0e", "type": "concept", "title": "How researcher presence shapes data"}], "description": "The point is to make assumptions and field effects visible."}, {"id": "46e5adad-0da6-4c48-ac84-a5c89aa976cb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Decolonizing and feminist methods", "children": [{"id": "ee43ab69-84a1-4119-a056-7deff3018bf2", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial histories of knowledge collection"}, {"id": "14936726-91d9-4412-a034-85bc3cd2c69c", "type": "concept", "title": "Collaborative authority and co-analysis"}, {"id": "6b7657c6-a6cb-4acf-b095-3168b8fdee29", "type": "concept", "title": "Care, reciprocity, refusal, and repair"}], "description": "Students examine critiques of extractive research."}]}, {"id": "2103cef0-d69c-4d01-902a-48106c9316f9", "type": "topic", "title": "Participant Observation", "description": "Participant observation is the signature ethnographic method. Students learn how to observe, participate, build rapport, and notice patterns without pretending to be invisible.", "children": [{"id": "76929e57-6829-42ed-9004-0d7717f5e455", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Observation strategies", "children": [{"id": "ee73da8f-421d-45ba-8958-a94ca127d6ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive, focused, and selective observation"}, {"id": "60cdcda5-112e-4c32-90da-06448bb2c76e", "type": "concept", "title": "Settings, actors, activities, objects, time, goals, and feelings"}, {"id": "47d91a37-c8a2-464b-ba64-54af68d7b919", "type": "concept", "title": "Mapping spaces, routines, and interaction patterns"}], "description": "Students practice turning ordinary scenes into systematic field data."}, {"id": "8f4b2a47-893f-4f32-891b-56d11c9b8910", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Participation and rapport", "children": [{"id": "23afb583-4efc-47ea-931c-b5db80583d59", "type": "concept", "title": "Roles from observer to participant"}, {"id": "764c8a2c-249c-4a20-b1e9-594295f90a90", "type": "concept", "title": "Trust-building and boundaries"}, {"id": "b1b74536-ed41-4f59-b17b-cad4775923b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Managing disclosure, expectations, and conflict"}], "description": "The gist is to understand immersion as negotiated involvement."}, {"id": "74c4ed33-9630-42bd-8a46-f9ffe11ed1d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Analytic noticing", "children": [{"id": "f9e60c2a-007a-44ab-820f-c7b0796afe1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Rituals, routines, silences, contradictions, and exceptions"}, {"id": "c7ec13b8-d0bd-4066-aa2f-01c5048f5149", "type": "concept", "title": "Pattern recognition across events"}, {"id": "074f3757-7a20-4e07-8d0c-258cb52bc3b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Questions generated from observation"}], "description": "Students learn to move from seeing to interpreting."}]}, {"id": "af5a82e1-649f-4a65-bcd2-98c1eb636aa3", "type": "topic", "title": "Fieldnotes and Documentation", "description": "Fieldnotes are the working memory of ethnography. This topic teaches students to record, organize, protect, and analyze field experiences with rigor.", "children": [{"id": "4d9cbc4c-2b03-434f-bbe2-3960cab2b6d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Jottings and expanded notes", "children": [{"id": "c53c684f-eb25-49fe-a126-cd6e17a1cd51", "type": "concept", "title": "Headnotes, scratch notes, and same-day expansion"}, {"id": "f158c25c-eea5-41fb-941f-fb57b2ea9e2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Scene, dialogue, sequence, sensory detail, and context"}, {"id": "fb790458-d4b2-461e-8239-dcfbb8f5e2db", "type": "concept", "title": "Distinguishing description from interpretation"}], "description": "Students learn note-taking rhythms that preserve detail without disrupting the field."}, {"id": "e1216574-66a8-423e-85c1-8068124efb45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reflexive and analytic memos", "children": [{"id": "9f7cea70-e6a1-4df0-8b90-345361009249", "type": "concept", "title": "Method memos, code memos, theory memos, and emotion memos"}, {"id": "70591a1b-e20e-4c66-916e-22f2d1e50f2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Negative cases and surprises"}, {"id": "29141333-307f-4726-a21d-60470cad3f9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Versioning ideas across time"}], "description": "The point is to document emerging ideas as data collection unfolds."}, {"id": "20275c87-0660-4334-be50-e929c39611a7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Documenting multimodal data", "children": [{"id": "a212318f-553c-496b-afb8-e2eded4e7171", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent for recording and images"}, {"id": "4cf80edf-fb59-41c0-a297-8fded0334a40", "type": "concept", "title": "Metadata, filenames, storage, and backups"}, {"id": "09683de2-c55f-4dbf-931d-38ff47351b11", "type": "concept", "title": "Captioning and contextual annotation"}], "description": "Students learn when and how to use photos, audio, video, maps, and artifacts."}]}, {"id": "3511bbe4-3168-4b4f-9cf0-1c9ac4e04b94", "type": "topic", "title": "Interviewing and Oral Histories", "description": "Interviews help researchers understand experience, memory, meaning, and interpretation. This topic covers interview design, listening, probing, and oral-history ethics.", "children": [{"id": "d3b8732b-84ae-4281-8760-69e022c15eb4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interview design", "children": [{"id": "c28c2aa1-f116-4949-841a-4e6e79f8bcc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Structured, semi-structured, unstructured, life-history, and expert interviews"}, {"id": "dade2dee-6666-478b-8f61-3216b1618811", "type": "concept", "title": "Question wording, sequence, probes, and follow-ups"}, {"id": "fb6cb4ca-3bcf-4b62-b06c-116be7b3baab", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilot interviews and protocol revision"}], "description": "Students learn to build protocols that guide without over-controlling conversation."}, {"id": "9d94eb87-60dc-49cc-bfc2-2684c2cca2e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interview practice", "children": [{"id": "47b67f36-3dae-4da8-8b97-08323fb0986a", "type": "concept", "title": "Rapport, pacing, silence, and active listening"}, {"id": "a51339b8-d81f-4b00-bab8-6bdb1b7894c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Recording, transcription, translation, and interpretation"}, {"id": "1570815d-e638-43dc-8810-9aa1e90bb674", "type": "concept", "title": "Handling sensitive topics and distress"}], "description": "The gist is to develop listening as a research skill."}, {"id": "7af3f25f-f9d2-46f7-8a22-080ab8c26c70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Oral history and memory", "children": [{"id": "68b41c2e-706d-4b76-b3d2-f58ba649dc50", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrative structure and life-course context"}, {"id": "3de9cce9-b9bc-45f0-a259-5ecd18e1c2cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective memory and historical trauma"}, {"id": "293d9bd2-49a0-42c8-8a27-38ca39fc05f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Rights, archives, and participant review"}], "description": "Students learn how memory is evidence, interpretation, and performance."}]}, {"id": "7659d30a-8c29-4d53-9953-fbbb5921c728", "type": "topic", "title": "Focus Groups, Elicitation, and Participatory Techniques", "description": "Some questions are best explored through group discussion, visual prompts, or collaborative activity. This topic adds methods that surface interaction and shared interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "28ffcbf6-e0e3-4f86-adaa-83ae245b47ff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Focus groups", "children": [{"id": "59f6c72c-a9f7-4beb-8eb7-b08423bc8d27", "type": "concept", "title": "Recruitment, group composition, facilitation, and ground rules"}, {"id": "83e6dcc0-00cb-499b-b356-13f1db743fe0", "type": "concept", "title": "Consensus, disagreement, status, and silence"}, {"id": "9848a55b-9bcc-4e67-9b6e-ca50424f90c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Confidentiality limits in group settings"}], "description": "Students learn how group data differ from individual interview data."}, {"id": "d9f35d94-2e94-4ddd-b5ed-09806bfe1bc7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Elicitation techniques", "children": [{"id": "4f764166-0957-487c-9b9e-b0e4781e3873", "type": "concept", "title": "Photo elicitation, object elicitation, free listing, pile sorting, and mapping"}, {"id": "2a109024-f67c-4a4c-9566-986c95d91059", "type": "concept", "title": "Cognitive anthropology and domain analysis"}, {"id": "74cbfd3a-db30-489b-bbac-b085268e5934", "type": "concept", "title": "When prompts reveal assumptions that direct questions miss"}], "description": "The point is to use prompts that help participants explain meanings and categories."}, {"id": "06f342dc-626b-465a-86aa-1faba929bba5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Participatory methods", "children": [{"id": "53009aab-1032-43e1-8b3b-e1e4ff15e822", "type": "concept", "title": "Participatory action research"}, {"id": "354850be-b59e-46f4-9f77-802ad28b349f", "type": "concept", "title": "Community mapping, timelines, workshops, and feedback sessions"}, {"id": "2da0e915-4ae0-45a3-936f-e40bc5583689", "type": "concept", "title": "Shared ownership, compensation, and decision-making"}], "description": "Students practice designing research with people rather than only about them."}]}, {"id": "cf9d1f6b-c3c0-4c04-b6d9-2361d976df8c", "type": "topic", "title": "Surveys, Measurement, and Mixed Methods", "description": "Anthropologists often need structured data alongside ethnography. This topic teaches careful survey design, measurement, and integration without reducing culture to numbers alone.", "children": [{"id": "7cb83c4d-2e13-43fd-9357-ab1b69f3aef2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Questionnaire design", "children": [{"id": "418b42d7-35d1-4cff-b277-88a1ffabea86", "type": "concept", "title": "Closed, open, Likert, ranking, and demographic items"}, {"id": "57d97368-9b77-45d4-af5a-1a74a0aed7d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Reliability, validity, translation, and cultural equivalence"}, {"id": "3e4d8338-156d-4e9f-ab66-62d63dbb6b3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilot testing and cognitive interviewing"}], "description": "Students learn to write survey items that produce interpretable data."}, {"id": "1b5b079b-5d66-43c2-a8e4-f45b80bf9bb0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling and administration", "children": [{"id": "431f099b-5ae9-45d7-9544-3635d192cb81", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling frames and recruitment channels"}, {"id": "5eb1cd53-8afd-4cb7-b285-a2b5e3e948b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Online, paper, interviewer-administered, and mobile surveys"}, {"id": "0fd3477b-5705-4cc3-8d3b-d06b2b7af8b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Response rates, missing data, and nonresponse bias"}], "description": "The goal is to understand who the survey reaches and who it misses."}, {"id": "fa322727-6aab-4e6e-b5cb-594e1a956ede", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Integrating survey and ethnographic data", "children": [{"id": "9ed667c8-c387-4b6a-966e-97ab05755e9e", "type": "concept", "title": "Explaining survey patterns through interviews"}, {"id": "7517d1de-abac-4825-beeb-89fd8792cea2", "type": "concept", "title": "Using ethnography to design measures"}, {"id": "90b82d48-2e2c-4115-8066-c10c4984b41f", "type": "concept", "title": "Joint displays and mixed-method inference"}], "description": "Students learn to connect patterns with meanings."}]}, {"id": "3b4980d8-be51-4d74-b4b3-c4870ce7038c", "type": "topic", "title": "Archival, Documentary, and Historical Methods", "description": "Anthropological fields include archives, bureaucratic records, media, and documents. This topic teaches students to treat texts and records as situated social artifacts.", "children": [{"id": "14c4f718-0471-45ab-b527-c1cd6db0aabb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Finding and evaluating archives", "children": [{"id": "e249dada-3b19-4716-a4e2-8a33fc5e44b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Finding aids, catalogs, special collections, and digital archives"}, {"id": "599dc587-3d5a-4912-8dd9-7f7936b3d5bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Provenance, silences, gaps, and classification systems"}, {"id": "f848cc0e-36cd-4606-8bb7-e65fb671438f", "type": "concept", "title": "Permissions, citation, and reproduction rights"}], "description": "Students learn how archival collections are produced, organized, and limited."}, {"id": "ced877be-bd00-4682-ab86-4586f17e6d7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Document analysis", "children": [{"id": "bc820a37-eae3-43af-9a96-64f29ec167a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy documents, case files, newspapers, reports, social media, and ephemera"}, {"id": "1da901fa-c562-4665-89f0-944b7725ad58", "type": "concept", "title": "Discourse, framing, and institutional voice"}, {"id": "93e07d0b-70c4-4c3a-8f81-aea6b2c1b26e", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing records with oral and observational data"}], "description": "The point is to read documents for content, genre, authorship, circulation, and power."}, {"id": "5c13c20a-8987-44ff-85d1-889093aaad3e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical anthropology", "children": [{"id": "0485ce30-868d-4d29-8a9d-2042894a90da", "type": "concept", "title": "Genealogies, timelines, memory, and event reconstruction"}, {"id": "e055c3d1-3230-4f29-855b-efdc70eff660", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial records and counter-archives"}, {"id": "2025286a-6c25-41bf-8185-b4de811091c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Present-day effects of historical processes"}], "description": "Students connect ethnographic questions to temporal depth."}]}, {"id": "90c5e38b-d4ec-4497-82fc-fee10008ac43", "type": "topic", "title": "Material Culture, Visual, Spatial, and Digital Methods", "description": "Anthropological evidence is not only spoken or written. This topic expands methods to objects, images, places, platforms, and digital traces.", "children": [{"id": "a5230149-da5f-4796-bf2e-990ed1e5554b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Material culture methods", "children": [{"id": "e053c891-31ba-40bc-a2ba-a74ed797a937", "type": "concept", "title": "Object biographies, use, exchange, repair, display, and disposal"}, {"id": "323b4a7c-0b43-4606-b27c-976a2bf05538", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, collections, and repatriation issues"}, {"id": "61a1d83d-4d7d-46ca-9f72-ec1274f9996f", "type": "concept", "title": "Materiality, embodiment, and everyday life"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze objects as social evidence."}, {"id": "6ac75f4c-f827-4616-aa6d-089dfb1312f7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visual and spatial methods", "children": [{"id": "d3eac812-9547-4eaf-9688-cfafe4051595", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography, film, sketching, mapping, and spatial walkthroughs"}, {"id": "63c413ec-645d-415a-830a-93d86b2497c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Participatory photography and visual consent"}, {"id": "e7ef5adf-fecc-4659-b7c6-c4c918d8e639", "type": "concept", "title": "GIS concepts, mental maps, and built-environment observation"}], "description": "The gist is to interpret images and places systematically."}, {"id": "c6acf456-a418-446b-b5fa-ed4d647b1250", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital ethnography", "children": [{"id": "794af549-ab46-4d02-b7e6-7843876d7452", "type": "concept", "title": "Platforms, communities, algorithms, and digital publics"}, {"id": "a8a215cf-21f8-43ea-9018-268d7d2fa2d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Screenshots, posts, metadata, and terms of service"}, {"id": "e6d9ed2c-61e5-4154-8f0e-ca93b3eb7b93", "type": "concept", "title": "Anonymity, traceability, and ethical quoting online"}], "description": "Students learn how online spaces require adapted fieldwork practices."}]}, {"id": "0d853479-a59c-4422-954c-6ca330e81ce2", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparative, Multi-Sited, and Team Research", "description": "Many anthropological questions cross locations, institutions, and scales. This topic helps students design comparative and collaborative projects without losing ethnographic depth.", "children": [{"id": "9c824b36-5f86-4e0e-8f94-9e76b85a6a7e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative logic", "children": [{"id": "0d9adb46-d502-428f-8827-62b1936d4f11", "type": "concept", "title": "Most-similar and most-different cases"}, {"id": "d900a12a-6752-48fe-8bc8-ca39fdc68be8", "type": "concept", "title": "Within-case and cross-case comparison"}, {"id": "da1fc79a-511a-4a0b-8cc7-0cded5b1a945", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding forced equivalence"}], "description": "Students learn how comparison can clarify variation and avoid overgeneralization."}, {"id": "2cb396d3-2111-46b4-bc47-29b45ccd9788", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multi-sited ethnography", "children": [{"id": "31699794-b357-4008-afc2-6efbf9de0a9c", "type": "concept", "title": "Follow the thing, metaphor, plot, conflict, biography, or policy"}, {"id": "ac392590-c742-461f-97c9-9d802e547eaf", "type": "concept", "title": "Scale, mobility, and transnational fields"}, {"id": "5b0dcb78-0817-4967-83e7-b5ae441e3e5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Managing uneven access across sites"}], "description": "The point is to follow people, objects, ideas, media, money, and policies across settings."}, {"id": "adc80599-636f-4c6b-be45-8f954292e70b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Team research", "children": [{"id": "bcb05fb6-3694-4cce-b80f-7f9dc87a024e", "type": "concept", "title": "Shared protocols and codebooks"}, {"id": "a575b8c0-e141-4a9a-8470-9ce1630f2572", "type": "concept", "title": "Intercoder discussion and calibration"}, {"id": "455eebec-acbb-448f-ae21-6db7006d3316", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship, labor, and project management"}], "description": "Students learn practices for collaborative rigor."}]}, {"id": "1128f1e0-802f-4e91-aaaa-692d1937004e", "type": "topic", "title": "Qualitative Coding and Analysis", "description": "Analysis begins during fieldwork and deepens after data collection. This topic teaches students to code, memo, compare, and build arguments from qualitative material.", "children": [{"id": "3ac433a6-1362-4d9f-9d9e-46b414968010", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Coding strategies", "children": [{"id": "8fa2b9ea-896e-47a7-8c92-2f8a9597b89f", "type": "concept", "title": "Open, descriptive, in vivo, process, values, and focused coding"}, {"id": "500d6616-58b5-4252-8053-9855be807777", "type": "concept", "title": "Codebooks, definitions, inclusion criteria, and examples"}, {"id": "dcfec99c-f5b8-407f-b616-d9d7bf35f6fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Iterative recoding and code consolidation"}], "description": "Students learn coding as disciplined interpretation, not mechanical labeling."}, {"id": "6f26a518-5fb5-4d3a-8b87-03947b8e46c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Thematic and interpretive analysis", "children": [{"id": "d20ec608-1475-4755-856b-9a8fcc96b278", "type": "concept", "title": "Themes, categories, typologies, and narratives"}, {"id": "b5f6b6da-7298-4139-9df7-c4417f913c1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Negative cases and rival explanations"}, {"id": "f73cbca6-6fa7-40e1-be22-ee06919d6b96", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description and analytic abstraction"}], "description": "The point is to move from codes to patterns, relationships, and claims."}, {"id": "06b6f7a2-a6a7-46fb-872e-60600ff8110e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Software-supported analysis", "children": [{"id": "00bc8d67-5cb4-4219-bf68-5276654ea709", "type": "concept", "title": "NVivo, MAXQDA, Dedoose, Atlas.ti, spreadsheets, and manual coding"}, {"id": "fe71fe27-8415-4ae7-b4cb-8db24fe90b74", "type": "concept", "title": "Queries, matrices, memos, and audit trails"}, {"id": "ea3922a0-4654-4635-bbd2-1acb0fd89924", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding software-driven superficial analysis"}], "description": "Students learn what tools can and cannot do."}]}, {"id": "7b5cdef3-bde9-4fbd-b665-0bfb27afd9df", "type": "topic", "title": "Quantitative Data, Statistics, and Visualization for Anthropologists", "description": "Anthropological researchers need enough quantitative fluency to interpret surveys, demographics, networks, and mixed-method evidence. This topic emphasizes practical, transparent analysis.", "children": [{"id": "8bf3b0b4-bedf-4786-93d5-ff3a0b32255f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Descriptive statistics", "children": [{"id": "08345334-6450-4223-bbae-d49ec5dd10be", "type": "concept", "title": "Counts, percentages, means, medians, distributions, and cross-tabs"}, {"id": "f135d5a6-671f-4496-98ea-51cd0d0a5943", "type": "concept", "title": "Missing data and outliers"}, {"id": "461cd72a-a04a-4ecc-94f5-66fe4fbb7aee", "type": "concept", "title": "Small-sample caution"}], "description": "Students learn to summarize data without overstating what it proves."}, {"id": "75d1bb16-e65d-49f5-b645-ecf6b794bc45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Applied analysis", "children": [{"id": "152c380c-3e02-4fce-8b1d-aecbf94e5870", "type": "concept", "title": "Spreadsheets, R, SPSS, or Python at an introductory level"}, {"id": "dd8bec29-525a-4e6e-8d6a-48e0b8a1b7d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparisons, associations, simple regression concepts, and confidence intervals"}, {"id": "20f09342-5b43-40c8-a818-3fb727daf8b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Choosing analysis appropriate to measurement and sampling"}], "description": "The goal is basic competence with common tools and outputs."}, {"id": "b5932143-f91b-448f-8ca3-b5ab2b1b6fb6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visualization and communication", "children": [{"id": "4e942d00-2859-4a4e-bcf6-d220fa2b13f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Tables, bar charts, timelines, maps, and network diagrams"}, {"id": "7734b5a8-6f68-4a63-8475-c427b06c6b4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Designing for accuracy and accessibility"}, {"id": "469edb7e-3b9f-47d7-9a0c-23936b4d7d5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Integrating numbers with ethnographic excerpts"}], "description": "Students learn to make visuals that clarify rather than impress."}]}, {"id": "bf0acb9c-3820-4945-8132-f27f09909721", "type": "topic", "title": "Data Management, Transparency, and Open Science", "description": "World-class methods require data stewardship. This topic teaches students to organize, protect, document, and share research materials responsibly.", "children": [{"id": "7b6812d5-d646-4455-b437-c0f342f3acfc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Data management planning", "children": [{"id": "0da98690-81fb-49f6-8e10-08cac9a7eb37", "type": "concept", "title": "File structures, naming conventions, backups, and encryption"}, {"id": "070644c1-82d7-4b2c-baeb-67e7c447594b", "type": "concept", "title": "Metadata, readme files, and research logs"}, {"id": "40a61cee-ca2b-441c-90d3-4562005f9b61", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensitive data, de-identification, and access controls"}], "description": "Students learn to plan storage, security, documentation, and retention before data collection."}, {"id": "6bb0319a-1046-4318-a409-e5ae061f4046", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Transparency and reproducibility", "children": [{"id": "af08e7bf-2105-4af0-b2b2-cb70d8d9945b", "type": "concept", "title": "Audit trails, memos, appendices, and methodological transparency"}, {"id": "a90d7fb2-907f-40aa-94a1-6d08a3717dba", "type": "concept", "title": "Preregistration debates in qualitative work"}, {"id": "0d0b8341-c4fc-44a8-8a10-7f8ae890c434", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotation for transparent inquiry and responsible data sharing"}], "description": "The point is to make interpretive research more auditable without flattening context."}, {"id": "619922a2-bdd3-4fa4-a04f-73141602880e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Repositories and long-term stewardship", "children": [{"id": "73499308-bf3f-4e7f-aa39-7c6b2987dd99", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualitative Data Repository and restricted access models"}, {"id": "0f78c1e3-43bf-4240-af8c-e68b8347c219", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent for archiving and reuse"}, {"id": "44ca651f-e2d8-4741-b7ed-9af6766dcb08", "type": "concept", "title": "Community data governance and refusal to share"}], "description": "Students learn when sharing data is useful, risky, or inappropriate."}]}, {"id": "90eace61-56c7-4b3a-823a-70440499cc7d", "type": "topic", "title": "Writing, Representation, and Evidence-Based Argument", "description": "Anthropological writing is a method of analysis and a public act. This topic teaches students to present evidence, interpretation, and ethical representation with care.", "children": [{"id": "e8ad4b99-cdea-4430-8bd2-5232511d6065", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Writing ethnographic description", "children": [{"id": "3885c6cc-ea6b-4562-ade0-70c2dfc907af", "type": "concept", "title": "Thick description and contextualization"}, {"id": "8686982f-9346-4ef0-bf86-d163e739c3d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Balancing narrative and argument"}, {"id": "d2e72313-8f4b-4614-961d-bf3097f27fec", "type": "concept", "title": "Composite cases, anonymization, and representational choices"}], "description": "Students learn how scenes, voices, and details support analysis."}, {"id": "4be083bb-95ae-4809-a7d1-a1352672182b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Building claims from evidence", "children": [{"id": "90f62e4d-2550-4000-a20c-9f7fa076738b", "type": "concept", "title": "Claim-evidence-reasoning structure"}, {"id": "0cc53141-795e-4d1c-a5a1-6786db6b7fa1", "type": "concept", "title": "Quoting fieldnotes and interviews responsibly"}, {"id": "f2d5959f-9369-41e2-ba88-b673b5c97e4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Handling contradiction and ambiguity"}], "description": "The gist is to connect data excerpts to defensible analytic points."}, {"id": "39f9b86c-d97a-4518-bcb1-f7f048073a88", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public and multimodal communication", "children": [{"id": "06de37c3-36ae-4b12-a783-16dacf1722ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy briefs, public essays, exhibits, podcasts, and digital projects"}, {"id": "1642cb26-d103-4e09-b00a-719c0b9caf81", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, accessibility, and translation"}, {"id": "6cd4a445-4f77-4b5e-b5f6-00721f54c95e", "type": "concept", "title": "Avoiding extraction, sensationalism, and deficit framing"}], "description": "Students practice communicating research beyond the term paper."}]}, {"id": "33f19362-62e3-4a01-a8d4-0e1b9ba1161b", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone: Research Proposal and Methods Portfolio", "description": "The capstone brings the course together. Students design a small, ethical, feasible anthropology project and assemble the materials needed to carry it out.", "children": [{"id": "daa399e1-4480-4511-91de-51350271456a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Proposal architecture", "children": [{"id": "f7dcb50f-215e-4985-821d-f8b016b512d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question, literature rationale, theory, site, sample, and methods"}, {"id": "fcc412cd-3a10-4808-8b18-ec2f4f4567d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics, risk, consent, and data management plan"}, {"id": "de71c1be-e641-474d-a14f-5b98269f69dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Timeline, feasibility, and expected contribution"}], "description": "Students learn the components of a research proposal."}, {"id": "ab3da620-f140-43e5-9a78-81eb2d019a6d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Methods portfolio", "children": [{"id": "f0eb2570-d2aa-4e62-acc1-52e6ab3f0fba", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation guide, interview protocol, survey or elicitation activity"}, {"id": "7078c7f9-1923-4c55-8d7c-de42e0af5728", "type": "concept", "title": "Fieldnote template, coding plan, and data organization plan"}, {"id": "68df617b-40ae-425d-835d-25a426ff2a6a", "type": "concept", "title": "Recruitment script, consent language, and reflexive memo"}], "description": "The point is to produce practical research instruments."}, {"id": "4bda7585-84ac-436a-837d-ed0321fde606", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Peer review and revision", "children": [{"id": "3bcacfe6-ff22-4065-a83e-46cdb8c3044c", "type": "concept", "title": "Methods defense presentation"}, {"id": "6d7dc562-952c-40b6-910c-d9663e762350", "type": "concept", "title": "Peer feedback on ethics and feasibility"}, {"id": "b5bbd905-0925-40f3-9c4f-0d775e461bd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision memo explaining design choices"}], "description": "Students learn that good research design improves through critique."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$rmanjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
